# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from lib import *

REL_AUD = ['white-label-account-recovery', 'how-white-label-account-recovery-works']

mk('white-label-recovery-for-social-media-agencies', 'For social media agencies', 'audience',
 'White Label Recovery for Social Media Agencies',
 'How a social media agency can offer account recovery under its own brand: typical lockouts, the handoff, and what to collect from the client.',
 'White label recovery for social media agencies.',
 'You run the client’s feed every day, so when the account goes dark, you are the one they call. Here is how to have an answer ready.',
 'Social media agencies are the first call when a client account is disabled, hacked or locked out. White label recovery lets you answer with a service of your own: you keep the client and the calendar, and Unbanz handles the platform appeal behind the scenes. The platform decides.',
 [('You are the first call', 'Clients blame the agency when posting stops, even when the cause is a platform notice.'),
  ('Protect the calendar', 'A quick, organised response keeps scheduled content and client trust intact.'),
  ('No new hire', 'You do not need a recovery specialist on payroll to offer this.')],
 [('situations', 'The lockouts social media agencies actually meet',
   UL(['A client Instagram account disabled after a scheduled post or a sudden wave of follows.',
       'A Facebook Page unpublished or restricted, which stops all scheduled content.',
       'A hacked account where the email and number were changed and the agency lost its own access.',
       'A login blocked by a code sent to a phone number the client no longer has.',
       'A brand handle that the client wants and a stranger holds.']) +
   P('Each one has a different official route. [[white-label-instagram-account-recovery|Instagram]], [[white-label-facebook-account-recovery|Facebook]] and [[white-label-hacked-account-recovery|hacked account]] pages explain the routes.')),
  ('handoff', 'How the handoff fits your day',
   OL(['Pause scheduled posts that could make things worse, and note what was live when the notice appeared.',
       'Ask the client for the notice screenshot and the date, and for their go-ahead for you to act.',
       'Send the details to Unbanz. You do not have to name the client or the company.',
       'Tell the client, in your words, that a specialist route has been started and that the platform decides.',
       'Update the client with the [[client-status-update-templates-account-recovery|status templates]] so you are not writing from scratch.'])),
  ('collect', 'What to keep on file for every client',
   UL(['Who owns the account and who the admins are.', 'The login email and whether the client still has access to it.', 'The phone number on the account and who holds it.', 'Whether two-factor authentication is on, and where the backup codes are.']) +
   P('This is the same list as the [[prevent-client-account-lockouts-agency-checklist|lockout prevention checklist]]. Collect it at onboarding and the first lockout becomes shorter.')),
  ('selling', 'Fitting it into your offer',
   P('Social media agencies usually package this in one of three ways: as an included safety net in a retainer, as an add-on called something like account protection, or as a pay-per-incident service. None of these needs you to promise a result. See [[agency-pricing-models-reselling-recovery|pricing models for reselling recovery]].')),
  ('tone', 'What to say to the client',
   TMPL('Short message for the first reply', 'I have seen the notice and I am starting the recovery process now. I will need a screenshot of the notice and the date it appeared. Please do not try random logins or share any codes. I will update you as soon as there is movement. The platform makes the final decision, so I will not promise an outcome, but I will keep you informed at each step.') +
   P('More wording in [[how-to-tell-client-account-disabled-template|how to tell a client their account is disabled]].'))],
 [('Why should a social media agency offer account recovery?', 'Clients call the agency first when an account stops working. Having a clear service to offer keeps the client with you and protects the content calendar.'),
  ('Do I have to hire a recovery specialist?', 'No. With white label, Unbanz does the platform work behind the scenes while you stay the contact. You handle intake and client updates.'),
  ('What should I collect from a client before contacting Unbanz?', 'The username or profile link, an unedited screenshot of the notice and its date, the email and number on the account and the client’s go-ahead. Never ask for a password.'),
  ('Can I include recovery in my retainer?', 'Yes, many agencies do, usually as a defined add-on. Do not promise a result, because the platform decides every appeal.'),
  ('What if the client’s account cannot be recovered?', 'Tell them plainly, and offer the next best step, such as setting up a new account correctly. The platform’s decision is final for that request.')],
 REL_AUD + ['white-label-recovery-for-community-managers-freelancers', 'agency-pricing-models-reselling-recovery'],
 'Social media agencies: the lockouts they meet and a handoff that fits the working day.')

mk('white-label-recovery-for-ad-agencies', 'For ad agencies', 'audience',
 'White Label Recovery for Ad Agencies and Media Buyers',
 'Paid media agencies lose spend when ad accounts are restricted. How to offer ad account and business portfolio recovery under your own brand.',
 'White label recovery for ad agencies.',
 'When an ad account is restricted, spend stops and the client wants answers. Here is how an ad agency can respond without building a recovery desk.',
 'For an ad agency, a restricted or disabled ad account stops campaigns and puts the retainer under pressure. White label ad account recovery lets you respond under your own brand while Unbanz prepares the appeal behind the scenes. Meta decides the outcome, so no result can be promised.',
 [('Campaigns stop', 'A disabled ad account pauses delivery. Clients notice within hours.'),
  ('Many assets, one problem', 'The restriction may sit on the ad account, the business portfolio, a Page or a profile.'),
  ('Honest answers', 'You report where the appeal stands. You do not predict the outcome.')],
 [('where', 'Where the restriction really sits',
   P('Ad agencies often start by assuming the ad account is the problem. In practice the restriction can sit on the ad account, the business portfolio, a Facebook Page or a personal profile that holds a role. Meta’s Account Quality area shows what is limited. Collect a screenshot of it before you contact anyone. The detail is in [[white-label-facebook-ad-account-recovery|white label Facebook ad account recovery]].')),
  ('table', 'What to note before the handoff',
   TABLE(['Item', 'Why it matters'],
         [['The exact wording of the restriction', 'Different notices lead to different appeal routes'],
          ['The date it appeared and what ran just before', 'Helps the appeal explain the situation honestly'],
          ['Which assets show a warning', 'A Page, ad account or portfolio can each need its own request'],
          ['Who the admins are on the business portfolio', 'The right person has to be able to complete checks'],
          ['What the business sells', 'Some categories carry extra rules']])),
  ('agency-side', 'What stays with the agency',
   UL(['The client conversation, including the hard one about paused spend.', 'The decision on whether to move budget to other accounts that are in good standing.', 'Any change to creative or landing pages the notice points to.', 'The report to the client.']) +
   P('A recovery request is not a place for shortcuts. Do not open new accounts to dodge a restriction, and do not use another business’s assets to keep spending. That kind of workaround tends to make things worse, and it is not something Unbanz does.')),
  ('process', 'A handoff routine for the media buying team',
   OL(['Pause what is not already stopped and keep a record of live campaigns.', 'Open Account Quality and screenshot every notice.', 'Send the details to Unbanz with the date and the business details.', 'Keep the client updated with plain facts.', 'When there is a decision, update the plan for budgets.'])),
  ('related', 'Related pages for media buyers',
   P('Media buyers should also read [[white-label-meta-ad-account-recovery-media-buyers|white label Meta ad account recovery for media buyers]], [[white-label-business-portfolio-recovery|business portfolio recovery]] and the main [[/agency-ad-account-recovery/|agency ad account recovery]] page.'))],
 [('Can an ad agency offer ad account recovery under its own name?', 'Yes. With white label, Unbanz prepares the appeal behind the scenes while the agency stays the contact and keeps the client relationship.'),
  ('What should an ad agency collect first?', 'A screenshot of the Account Quality notices, the date, the admins on the business portfolio, and the client’s go-ahead. Never a password.'),
  ('Will recovery restore my campaigns?', 'Nobody can promise that. Meta decides the appeal, and campaigns only run again if the restriction is lifted.'),
  ('Should I open a new ad account while I wait?', 'Do not use new accounts to dodge a restriction. It can make matters worse. Talk to the client about moving budget only to accounts in good standing.'),
  ('Does the client see Unbanz?', 'No. Unbanz works for the agency behind the scenes and never contacts the client.')],
 ['white-label-facebook-ad-account-recovery', 'white-label-meta-ad-account-recovery-media-buyers', 'white-label-business-portfolio-recovery', 'white-label-account-recovery'],
 'Ad agencies: where the restriction sits and a handoff that protects spend.')

mk('white-label-recovery-for-reputation-management-firms', 'For reputation management firms', 'audience',
 'White Label Recovery for Reputation Management',
 'Reputation management firms handle fake accounts, impersonation and lost profiles. How to hand the platform work to a partner under your brand.',
 'White label recovery for reputation management firms.',
 'Your clients hire you to protect a name online. When a profile is disabled or an impostor shows up, you need an answer that stays under your brand.',
 'Reputation management firms deal with lost profiles, impostors and unwanted accounts. White label recovery lets such a firm hand the appeal, impersonation report or username claim to Unbanz while the firm stays the only contact. Nothing is promised, because the platform decides every request.',
 [('Profiles and impostors', 'The two problems that land on a reputation desk most often.'),
  ('Quiet work', 'Reputation clients value discretion. White label keeps the extra party invisible.'),
  ('Official routes only', 'No pressure tactics, no mass reports. Only the platform’s own forms.')],
 [('problems', 'The platform problems on a reputation desk',
   UL(['A client profile disabled or restricted right when it matters.', 'A fake account using the client’s name and photos.', 'A hacked account posting things the client never wrote.', 'A brand handle held by someone else.']) +
   P('Each has an official route. Impersonation has its own report form, hacked accounts have the hacked-account tools, and disabled accounts have an appeal. See [[white-label-impersonation-fake-account-removal|white label impersonation and fake account removal]].')),
  ('discretion', 'Discretion by design',
   P('Reputation work is sensitive. With white label you decide what the client is told, and Unbanz works with you alone. You can send the username and the notice without naming the client or the firm’s other engagements. Read [[agency-client-confidentiality-account-recovery|client confidentiality for recovery work]] for how to handle the details.')),
  ('limits', 'Where reputation firms must stay careful',
   UL(['Do not file reports against accounts you only dislike. Reports must describe a real breach of the platform’s rules.', 'Do not organise mass reporting. It breaks the rules and can backfire on your client.', 'Do not promise removal of a negative review or critical post. Criticism that follows the rules does not come down.']) +
   P('Unbanz helps with real impersonation and rule-breaking accounts, not with silencing critics.')),
  ('flow', 'A workflow that fits a reputation firm',
   OL(['Document the offending or lost account: URL, screenshot, date.', 'State clearly what rule is broken or what access is lost.', 'Send the details to Unbanz.', 'Keep a log of what was filed and when, so the client report is accurate.', 'Report the platform’s answer in plain words.']))],
 [('What can a reputation management firm hand over?', 'Disabled or hacked profiles, impersonation accounts and brand handles. All go through the platform’s official routes.'),
  ('Can you remove negative posts about my client?', 'No. Posts that follow the platform’s rules stay up. Only accounts or content that break the rules can be reported, and the platform decides.'),
  ('Will the client know a partner is involved?', 'No. Unbanz works for your firm behind the scenes and does not contact the client.'),
  ('Do you use mass reporting?', 'No. Mass or fake reports break the rules and are never used. Each request is a single, accurate report or appeal.'),
  ('Do I need to name the client?', 'No. Send the username, a screenshot and the date. A name is not needed.')],
 REL_AUD + ['white-label-impersonation-fake-account-removal', 'agency-client-confidentiality-account-recovery', 'white-label-recovery-for-pr-firms'],
 'Reputation firms: profiles, impostors and discretion, with clear limits.')

mk('white-label-recovery-for-influencer-talent-agencies', 'For influencer and talent agencies', 'audience',
 'White Label Recovery for Influencer Agencies',
 'Influencer and talent agencies depend on their creators’ accounts. How to offer recovery under your own brand and keep the talent relationship.',
 'White label recovery for influencer and talent agencies.',
 'A creator’s account is their income, and yours. When one goes down, the agency needs a calm, ready response.',
 'For an influencer or talent agency, a creator’s disabled or hacked account stops sponsored work and shakes trust. White label recovery lets the agency respond under its own brand while Unbanz handles the platform appeal behind the scenes. The talent only deals with the agency, and the platform decides.',
 [('Income is at stake', 'Brand deals depend on live accounts. Delays hurt both creator and agency.'),
  ('Trust matters', 'Talent expects their agency to know what to do.'),
  ('Quiet partner', 'The creator deals with their agent, not with a third party.')],
 [('why', 'Why talent accounts get locked',
   UL(['Large audiences attract hacking attempts, phishing messages and impersonators.', 'Rapid growth and busy posting can trip automated systems.', 'Creators use many accounts and collaborators, which raises the risk of a lost login.', 'Brand deals involve shared access with managers and editors.']) +
   P('The platform’s own decision is final every time, and the guidance is the same for every account type.')),
  ('table', 'Typical talent problems and the usual route',
   TABLE(['Problem', 'Usual official route'],
         [['Disabled or suspended account', 'In-app review or appeal'],
          ['Hacked, email or number changed', 'The hacked-account tools on a device used before'],
          ['Impostor account using the creator’s name', 'The impersonation report'],
          ['Handle the creator wants', 'Trademark report if registered, or a recovery route if it was theirs before'],
          ['Brand collaboration ad access lost', 'Account Quality and business portfolio roles']])),
  ('agency', 'What the agency keeps',
   P('You keep the relationship with the creator and with the brands who book them. You decide what the creator is told, what the brand is told and whether to pause a campaign. Unbanz does not contact either side. For the brand side, a plain note about a pause is usually enough, and you can borrow wording from [[client-status-update-templates-account-recovery|the status update templates]].')),
  ('intake', 'Information to collect from the creator',
   UL(['The username and the email and number on the account.', 'Screenshots of the notice and any emails from the platform.', 'The date the account was last used normally.', 'Whether the account is also linked to a business portfolio or a Page.', 'Who else has access, such as editors or assistants.']) +
   P('Use the [[agency-intake-form-checklist-account-recovery|intake checklist]] and never ask for a password.')),
  ('prep', 'Reducing the chance of a repeat',
   P('After any recovery, help the creator turn on two-factor authentication with an authenticator app, store backup codes somewhere safe and remove old collaborators. The [[prevent-client-account-lockouts-agency-checklist|prevention checklist]] has the full list.'))],
 [('Why would a talent agency use white label recovery?', 'It lets the agency respond to a creator’s lockout under its own brand without building the skill in-house, and it keeps the creator relationship with the agency.'),
  ('Does the creator find out about Unbanz?', 'No. Unbanz works for the agency behind the scenes and does not contact creators.'),
  ('What if a creator’s account is gone for good?', 'The platform’s decision is final for that request. Tell the creator plainly and plan around a new account or other channels.'),
  ('Can you claim a handle for a creator?', 'Sometimes, through official routes such as a trademark report or a recovery route when the handle was the creator’s before. No handle can be promised.'),
  ('Do you need the creator’s password?', 'Never. Passwords and login codes stay with the creator.')],
 REL_AUD + ['white-label-hacked-account-recovery', 'white-label-username-claim', 'white-label-recovery-for-ugc-agencies'],
 'Talent agencies: why creator accounts get locked and how to respond.')

mk('white-label-recovery-for-pr-firms', 'For PR firms', 'audience',
 'White Label Recovery for PR Firms',
 'PR firms manage brand and executive profiles under time pressure. How a PR firm can add account recovery under its own name without new staff.',
 'White label recovery for PR firms.',
 'A disabled brand account in the middle of a launch is a PR problem first. Here is how to give the client a competent answer.',
 'A PR firm often runs the public accounts of brands and executives, so a disabled or hacked profile during a launch is urgent. White label recovery lets the firm answer under its own name while Unbanz handles the platform appeal in the background. The platform decides, and no outcome is promised.',
 [('Launch risk', 'A locked account during an announcement costs attention you cannot get back.'),
  ('Executive profiles', 'High-profile people are targeted by impersonators and phishing.'),
  ('Consistent voice', 'The client hears from one trusted contact, the PR firm.')],
 [('moments', 'When recovery becomes a PR issue',
   UL(['A brand account disabled right before an announcement.', 'An executive profile hacked and posting scam links.', 'A fake account imitating the company and answering customers.', 'A Page unpublished during a media cycle.']) +
   P('In each moment, the first hour is about communication. The platform appeal is a parallel track that Unbanz can run for you.')),
  ('parallel', 'Two tracks, run in parallel',
   TABLE(['Track', 'Who runs it', 'What it covers'],
         [['Communication', 'The PR firm', 'Holding statements, client updates, alternative channels'],
          ['Platform appeal', 'Unbanz, behind the scenes', 'The appeal, hacked-account tools or impersonation report through official routes']])),
  ('statements', 'What to say publicly while the account is down',
   P('Keep it short and factual: the account is temporarily unavailable, here is where updates will appear. Do not blame the platform and do not promise a date. Do not mention the recovery partner. The private client briefing can be plainer.') +
   TMPL('Internal client briefing, short form', 'The account is disabled and the appeal is filed. We do not know the platform’s timing. Until then, announcements go out on the website and the newsletter. We will update you at each step and will not predict the outcome.')),
  ('confidential', 'Confidential material',
   P('PR firms often work under confidentiality terms. Send only what the appeal needs: the username, the notice and its date. You do not need to share campaign plans or internal documents. Read [[agency-client-confidentiality-account-recovery|client confidentiality and NDAs]] before you send anything sensitive.')),
  ('after', 'After the decision',
   P('Brief the client, then tighten the account: second admin, authenticator app, recovery email owned by the company. See [[prevent-client-account-lockouts-agency-checklist|the prevention checklist]].'))],
 [('Why would a PR firm use white label recovery?', 'PR firms need a quick way to respond to a disabled or hacked client account. White label lets them answer under their own name without building the skill themselves.'),
  ('Can Unbanz restore an executive’s hacked profile?', 'Unbanz can prepare and file through the official hacked-account route. The platform decides, and no outcome can be promised.'),
  ('Should the public statement mention the recovery partner?', 'No. Keep the statement to what the client’s audience needs to know, and keep partner details private.'),
  ('Do I have to share campaign details?', 'No. Only the username, the notice screenshot, the date and the client’s go-ahead.'),
  ('What about impersonators?', 'There is an official impersonation report. See the white label impersonation page for the route and its limits.')],
 REL_AUD + ['white-label-recovery-for-reputation-management-firms', 'agency-client-confidentiality-account-recovery', 'client-status-update-templates-account-recovery'],
 'PR firms: a communication track and a platform track, run in parallel.')

mk('white-label-recovery-for-ecommerce-agencies', 'For e-commerce and dropshipping agencies', 'audience',
 'White Label Recovery for E-commerce Agencies',
 'E-commerce and dropshipping agencies depend on shop social accounts and ad accounts. How to offer recovery under your brand and protect sales channels.',
 'White label recovery for e-commerce and dropshipping agencies.',
 'For an online shop, the social account and the ad account are sales channels. When one stops, revenue stops with it.',
 'E-commerce and dropshipping agencies rely on a shop’s Instagram, Facebook Page and ad account for traffic. When one is disabled, sales drop. White label recovery lets the agency offer an appeal service under its own brand while Unbanz handles the platform work. The platform decides the outcome.',
 [('Sales channels', 'A disabled Page or ad account can cut the traffic a shop depends on.'),
  ('Ad and shop link', 'Catalogues, pixels and shop tabs hang off the same accounts.'),
  ('Honest, calm updates', 'Shop owners want facts and a plan B, not promises.')],
 [('exposure', 'Where an online shop is exposed',
   TABLE(['Asset', 'What stops if it is lost'],
         [['Instagram account', 'Organic traffic, shoppable posts, creator partnerships'],
          ['Facebook Page', 'Page posts, the shop tab, ad identity'],
          ['Ad account', 'All paid traffic and retargeting'],
          ['Business portfolio', 'Access to all of the above for the team']])),
  ('policy', 'Why e-commerce notices are common',
   P('Shops sell products, and products are subject to commerce and advertising rules. A notice may point to a product category, a claim in an ad or a mismatch between a store and its ads. The wording of the notice matters, so keep the screenshot. Do not edit ads or products in a rush without reading it, because changes can complicate the appeal. See [[white-label-facebook-ad-account-recovery|ad account recovery]] for how those appeals are structured.')),
  ('dropshipping', 'A note for dropshipping agencies',
   P('Dropshipping shops often run on thin setups: a new Page, a fresh ad account and quick scaling. That is precisely where restrictions land. Unbanz handles real appeals for real businesses through the official process. It does not buy, sell or rent accounts, does not provide replacement accounts, and does not help to dodge a restriction.')),
  ('plan-b', 'Giving the shop owner a plan B',
   UL(['Keep email and SMS lists up to date so a lost social account does not mean a lost audience.', 'Do not move all ad spend to one new account in a rush.', 'Make sure a second admin exists on every business asset.', 'Keep the notice screenshots and the date of each event in one folder.'])),
  ('handoff', 'The handoff',
   P('Send the notice, the date, the business name and the website to Unbanz, and keep the shop owner informed with short factual updates. A checklist is in [[agency-intake-form-checklist-account-recovery|the agency intake checklist]].'))],
 [('Can an e-commerce agency resell account recovery?', 'Yes. Under white label the agency stays the contact for the shop owner and Unbanz handles the platform work behind the scenes.'),
  ('Does this work for dropshipping stores?', 'Unbanz handles appeals for real businesses through official routes. It does not help dodge restrictions, supply accounts or buy and sell accounts.'),
  ('Should I edit ads while an appeal is open?', 'Read the notice first. Hasty changes can complicate an appeal, so decide with the facts in hand.'),
  ('What if the ad account is not restored?', 'The platform’s decision is final for that request. Discuss plan B with the shop owner, such as other channels and a second, properly set up account.'),
  ('Does the shop owner see Unbanz?', 'No. Unbanz works for your agency and never contacts the shop owner.')],
 REL_AUD + ['white-label-facebook-ad-account-recovery', 'white-label-business-portfolio-recovery', 'prevent-client-account-lockouts-agency-checklist'],
 'E-commerce agencies: shop channels at risk, policy notices and a plan B.')

mk('white-label-recovery-for-ugc-agencies', 'For UGC agencies', 'audience',
 'White Label Recovery for UGC Agencies',
 'UGC agencies work with many creator accounts and brand profiles. How to handle lockouts, hacked creator accounts and handle claims under your own brand.',
 'White label recovery for UGC agencies.',
 'A UGC agency touches dozens of accounts at once: brand profiles, creator profiles and ad access. Lockouts are a matter of when.',
 'UGC agencies manage content for brands through many creator accounts, so a disabled or hacked profile can delay deliveries. White label recovery lets a UGC agency offer appeal help under its own brand while Unbanz handles the platform side behind the scenes. The platform decides, and no outcome is promised.',
 [('Many accounts', 'More accounts means more notices, and more chances of a lost login.'),
  ('Delivery risk', 'A lockout can hold up content that a brand is waiting to post.'),
  ('Creator trust', 'Creators expect the agency to look after them.')],
 [('map', 'The account map of a UGC agency',
   TABLE(['Account', 'Owner', 'Risk'],
         [['Brand profile', 'The brand', 'Disabled or hacked during a campaign'],
          ['Creator profile', 'The creator', 'Hacked or disabled, hurting delivery'],
          ['Agency profile', 'The agency', 'Needs the same protections as any business'],
          ['Ad access for whitelisted content', 'The brand or the agency', 'Restricted ad account or lost permissions']])),
  ('who', 'Who the client is, situation by situation',
   P('In UGC the person who owns the account may be the creator, not the brand. Before sending anything, work out who owns the account and who gives the go-ahead. The account owner must agree to the appeal, because the platform may need to contact them. Add that to your creator agreement: a short line that you may arrange recovery help on their behalf if their account is locked.')),
  ('process', 'A routine for creator lockouts',
   OL(['The creator tells you and sends a screenshot of the notice.', 'You confirm what content was live for a brand and tell the brand a delay is possible.', 'You send the username, the notice and the date to Unbanz.', 'The creator completes any identity check the platform sends them.', 'You report back to the brand without revealing private details.'])),
  ('contracts', 'What to put in your creator agreements',
   P('Ask a lawyer to word it. In plain terms, include who may act on a locked account, what information the creator agrees to share and that the platform makes the final decision. See [[client-terms-account-recovery-clause|what to put in your client terms]] for plain suggestions that are not legal advice.')),
  ('safe', 'Keeping creators safe',
   P('Remind creators never to share logins with the agency by chat. Use platform roles and partner access where available. The [[prevent-client-account-lockouts-agency-checklist|lockout prevention checklist]] covers it.'))],
 [('Can a UGC agency offer recovery to its creators?', 'Yes. Under white label the agency is the contact for the creator and for the brand, and Unbanz handles the appeal behind the scenes.'),
  ('Who has to give the go-ahead?', 'The account owner, who is often the creator. The platform may contact them directly.'),
  ('What if a brand profile is locked, not the creator’s?', 'The same routine applies. Collect the notice, the date and the brand’s go-ahead, and send the details to Unbanz.'),
  ('Can you claim a handle for a creator brand?', 'Sometimes through official routes. No handle can be promised.'),
  ('Do creators see Unbanz?', 'No. Unbanz works for the agency and never contacts creators or brands.')],
 REL_AUD + ['white-label-recovery-for-influencer-talent-agencies', 'client-terms-account-recovery-clause', 'agency-intake-form-checklist-account-recovery'],
 'UGC agencies: many accounts, unclear owners and a routine for creator lockouts.')

mk('white-label-recovery-for-community-managers-freelancers', 'For community managers and freelancers', 'audience',
 'White Label Recovery for Freelance Social Managers',
 'Community managers and freelancers can offer account recovery without building the skill. How to package it, price it and stay honest about outcomes.',
 'White label recovery for community managers and freelancers.',
 'Working alone, you cannot spend a week learning appeal routes while your clients wait. A partner behind the scenes closes that gap.',
 'Community managers and freelancers often handle client accounts alone, so a lockout can swallow a whole week. White label recovery lets a solo professional offer account recovery under their own name while Unbanz handles the appeal behind the scenes. The platform decides, and no outcome can be promised.',
 [('You are the whole team', 'No one else to hand the problem to. A partner takes the platform work.'),
  ('Look established', 'Offering recovery shows clients you have the safety net covered.'),
  ('Stay in your lane', 'You do the client and content work. The partner does the appeal.')],
 [('solo', 'The solo professional’s problem',
   P('A freelancer who manages five or ten client accounts does not have time to become an appeal specialist, and each platform change means more homework. At the same time, clients assume the freelancer knows. Saying “I do not do that” is an honest answer, but it sends the client elsewhere. Saying “I will start the recovery process” keeps the client.')),
  ('package', 'Ways to package it',
   UL(['As a line in your service agreement: if an account is locked, you will start recovery with a specialist partner and the client pays separately.', 'As an add-on to a monthly package: account protection and recovery handling.', 'As a pay-per-incident service you mention when onboarding.']) +
   P('Pricing models are explained in [[agency-pricing-models-reselling-recovery|pricing models for reselling recovery]].')),
  ('kit', 'A small kit for a solo operator',
   OL(['A one-page checklist of what to ask a client at onboarding.', 'The [[agency-intake-form-checklist-account-recovery|intake checklist]] for a lockout.', 'Two or three message templates from [[client-status-update-templates-account-recovery|the status templates]].', 'A saved WhatsApp chat or email address for your partner.'])),
  ('honesty', 'Staying honest as a freelancer',
   P('Do not tell a client you have a contact at Meta. Do not promise a result. Do not take a client’s password. These three rules protect your reputation more than any marketing. Unbanz works the same way: official routes, honest answers, no insiders.')),
  ('growth', 'A small way to grow the business',
   P('Many clients never think about account security until it fails. Offering a short security check at onboarding creates a natural moment to mention recovery support. See [[prevent-client-account-lockouts-agency-checklist|the lockout prevention checklist]] for a ready-made list.'))],
 [('Can a freelancer use white label account recovery?', 'Yes. A solo community manager can offer recovery under their own name while Unbanz handles the platform work behind the scenes.'),
  ('Do I need a company to work with Unbanz?', 'Message us and say what you do. Terms are agreed before any work starts.'),
  ('How should I price it as a freelancer?', 'Price your time and coordination, plus the partner’s terms. Do not price an outcome, because the platform decides.'),
  ('What if a client asks who does the work?', 'Answer honestly. Many freelancers use specialist partners, and you can say a specialist handles the platform side.'),
  ('Will Unbanz contact my client?', 'No. Unbanz only talks to you.')],
 REL_AUD + ['white-label-recovery-for-social-media-agencies', 'become-a-white-label-recovery-partner', 'how-to-price-account-recovery-for-agencies'],
 'Freelancers and community managers: packaging, kit and staying honest.')

mk('white-label-recovery-for-app-growth-agencies', 'For app growth agencies', 'audience',
 'White Label Recovery for App Growth Agencies',
 'App growth and performance agencies rely on social and ad accounts to acquire users. How to offer recovery and protect acquisition channels.',
 'White label recovery for app growth agencies.',
 'App installs often come through social and Meta ads. Lose the account and acquisition stops. A recovery option keeps the growth plan on track.',
 'App growth agencies buy installs and subscribers through paid social, so a restricted ad account or disabled brand profile halts acquisition. White label recovery lets the agency respond under its own brand while Unbanz handles the platform appeal behind the scenes. The platform decides, and no outcome is promised.',
 [('Acquisition stops', 'Paid social is often the main install channel. A restriction pauses it.'),
  ('Metrics get noisy', 'A sudden stop makes cohort and creative tests hard to read.'),
  ('Account health is part of the plan', 'Good practice includes watching Account Quality regularly.')],
 [('channels', 'Where app growth meets account risk',
   UL(['Meta ad accounts that fund user acquisition.', 'App brand profiles on Instagram and Facebook used for organic and creator work.', 'Business portfolios shared across several apps.', 'Pages tied to app store listings and ad identity.']) +
   P('Ad account problems are covered on [[white-label-facebook-ad-account-recovery|white label Facebook ad account recovery]] and [[white-label-meta-ad-account-recovery-media-buyers|the media buyer guide]].')),
  ('monitor', 'Habits that reduce surprises',
   UL(['Check Account Quality on a schedule, not only after a problem.', 'Keep a list of which apps share which business portfolio, so one restriction does not catch you unprepared.', 'Keep creative approvals and claims within the platform’s ad policies.', 'Keep payment and verification details on every ad account current.']) +
   P('These habits do not prevent every restriction, but they shorten the time between a notice and a complete appeal.')),
  ('split', 'Who does what during an incident',
   TABLE(['Task', 'Agency', 'Unbanz'],
         [['Pause or reassign budget', 'Yes', 'No'],
          ['Document the notice and the dates', 'Yes', 'Checks it is complete'],
          ['Prepare and file the appeal', 'No', 'Yes'],
          ['Update the app client', 'Yes', 'No'],
          ['Decide the outcome', 'No', 'No']])),
  ('client-talk', 'Talking to the app client',
   P('App founders ask about install volume, not appeals. Lead with the effect: spend paused, plan in motion, next update at a set point. Avoid dates for a decision. [[client-status-update-templates-account-recovery|The status templates]] have wording for this.'))],
 [('Is this for performance marketing agencies too?', 'Yes. Any agency that runs paid social for a client can hand a restricted ad account to a white label partner.'),
  ('Does Unbanz run ads?', 'No. Unbanz handles account recovery and appeals. It does not run campaigns.'),
  ('Can you fix a business portfolio shared across apps?', 'Unbanz can prepare an appeal. The platform decides, and nothing is promised.'),
  ('Should I create a new ad account in the meantime?', 'Not as a way to dodge a restriction. Talk to the client about moving budget to accounts in good standing.'),
  ('Will the app client know about Unbanz?', 'No. Unbanz works for your agency behind the scenes.')],
 REL_AUD + ['white-label-recovery-for-ad-agencies', 'white-label-business-portfolio-recovery'],
 'App growth agencies: acquisition channels at risk and incident roles.')

mk('white-label-recovery-for-web-design-seo-agencies', 'For web design and SEO agencies', 'audience',
 'White Label Recovery for Web Design and SEO Agencies',
 'Web design and SEO agencies often set up clients’ social profiles and get blamed when they fail. How to add recovery support under your brand.',
 'White label recovery for web design and SEO agencies.',
 'You build the website and perhaps the profiles around it. When a client’s Instagram or Facebook stops working, you are still the person they call.',
 'Web design and SEO agencies often set up social profiles and Google profiles for clients, so they get the call when a social account is locked. White label recovery lets such an agency offer help under its own brand while Unbanz handles the platform side behind the scenes. The platform decides.',
 [('You set it up', 'Clients assume the person who built the profile can fix it.'),
  ('Add-on, not a pivot', 'Recovery support sits next to your main work. It does not change what you do.'),
  ('Keeps clients close', 'A helpful answer at the right moment protects the retainer.')],
 [('why', 'Why web and SEO agencies get these calls',
   P('Many agencies create the Facebook Page, link the Instagram account to it and connect the website. The client then sees the agency as the owner of the whole online presence. When a notice appears, the client asks the agency before asking anyone else.')),
  ('tie', 'How social account problems affect your own work',
   UL(['Broken links between the website and the Page or profile.', 'Social proof and embedded feeds that disappear from the site.', 'Business information that must match across the website, search listings and social profiles.', 'Ad and tracking setups tied to the same business portfolio.']) +
   P('A quick recovery plan protects your site and the surrounding work.')),
  ('offer', 'A modest way to offer it',
   OL(['Add a sentence about account recovery support to your care plans or terms.', 'Keep the [[agency-intake-form-checklist-account-recovery|intake checklist]] ready.', 'When a client reports a lockout, collect the details and pass them on.', 'Report to the client in your own words.'])),
  ('limits', 'What to avoid',
   UL(['Do not accept a client’s password to try to fix things yourself.', 'Do not promise that a recovery will work.', 'Do not claim to have a contact at Meta.']) +
   P('The same rules apply to every agency type. See [[agency-account-recovery-red-flags|red flags for agencies]].')),
  ('prep', 'Prevention as a service',
   P('A short social account security review at launch, covering admins, backup email and an authenticator app, is a natural fit for web agencies. The [[prevent-client-account-lockouts-agency-checklist|prevention checklist]] gives you the list.'))],
 [('Why would a web design agency offer recovery?', 'Clients assume the agency that set up their online presence can fix social account problems. A partner lets you answer without building the skill.'),
  ('Do I have to be a social media agency?', 'No. Any agency or freelancer whose clients lose access to an Instagram or Facebook account can use white label recovery.'),
  ('Can I include it in a care plan?', 'Yes. Describe it as support with a recovery process, and do not promise the result.'),
  ('Does the client see Unbanz?', 'No. Unbanz works only with your agency.'),
  ('What should I never take from a client?', 'A password or a login code. Use the platform’s own tools and roles.')],
 REL_AUD + ['white-label-recovery-for-local-marketing-agencies', 'prevent-client-account-lockouts-agency-checklist'],
 'Web and SEO agencies: why you get the call and a modest way to offer help.')

mk('white-label-recovery-for-branding-creative-studios', 'For branding and creative studios', 'audience',
 'White Label Recovery for Branding and Creative Studios',
 'Branding and creative studios hand over social profiles at the end of a project. How to offer handle claims and recovery support under your own studio name.',
 'White label recovery for branding and creative studios.',
 'A new brand needs a matching handle. A launched brand needs its profiles to stay online. Both are part of the studio’s job.',
 'Branding and creative studios choose names, build identities and often set up the social profiles for launch. White label recovery gives such a studio a way to claim a brand handle or recover a locked profile under its own name, while Unbanz handles the platform work behind the scenes. The platform decides.',
 [('Handles matter', 'A brand with the wrong handle loses consistency.'),
  ('Launch risk', 'A locked profile on launch week is a branding failure too.'),
  ('Studio voice', 'You present the service as part of your brand package.')],
 [('handles', 'Handles during brand creation',
   P('A strong name can be spoiled by a taken handle. Check availability early, and if the handle is held by an inactive or squatted account, know what the options are. A registered trademark that matches the handle is the strongest route. Without one, there is no public form for inactive handles, and honest providers say so. See [[white-label-username-claim|white label username claims]].')),
  ('table', 'Handle routes in plain terms',
   TABLE(['Situation', 'Usual route'],
         [['Registered trademark matches the handle', 'The platform’s intellectual property report'],
          ['An account is posing as the brand', 'The impersonation report'],
          ['The handle was the client’s before', 'Recovery of the old account first'],
          ['A neutral, unused handle', 'No sure route. Some handles cannot be obtained.']])),
  ('profiles', 'After launch',
   UL(['Make sure the client owns the accounts and the studio is only an admin.', 'Set a recovery email on the client’s own domain.', 'Document the roles so a lockout does not become an ownership argument.']) +
   P('See [[prevent-client-account-lockouts-agency-checklist|prevent lockouts]] for a full list.')),
  ('studio-offer', 'Packaging it as a studio',
   P('Studios can add handle research to a naming package and recovery support to a brand care plan. Keep the wording honest: you will start the process, and the platform decides. Pricing options are in [[agency-pricing-models-reselling-recovery|pricing models for reselling recovery]].')),
  ('handoff', 'The handoff',
   P('Send the desired handle, any trademark number and who the client is. If the client is not ready to be named, you do not have to name them. See [[agency-intake-form-checklist-account-recovery|the intake checklist]].'))],
 [('Can a studio claim a handle for a client brand?', 'Sometimes, through official routes such as a trademark report. No handle can be promised, and some have no honest route.'),
  ('Do you buy or sell handles?', 'No. Buying and selling accounts is not part of the service.'),
  ('What if the client wants a handle with no trademark?', 'There is no public form for inactive handles. Unbanz tells you honestly if there is a legitimate route.'),
  ('Does the client see Unbanz?', 'No. Unbanz works for your studio behind the scenes.'),
  ('Is there a refund policy?', 'Yes. Our refund policy is a 100% refund if a username claim fails.')],
 REL_AUD + ['white-label-username-claim', 'white-label-recovery-for-web-design-seo-agencies'],
 'Branding studios: handle routes, launch risks and a studio-friendly offer.')

mk('white-label-recovery-for-local-marketing-agencies', 'For local marketing agencies', 'audience',
 'White Label Recovery for Local Marketing Agencies',
 'Local marketing agencies serve small businesses that rarely understand platform notices. How to offer recovery support and keep the neighbourhood client.',
 'White label recovery for local marketing agencies.',
 'Your clients run salons, restaurants, clinics and shops. When their Facebook Page disappears, they call you, not Meta.',
 'Local marketing agencies serve small businesses whose Facebook Page or Instagram account is their shop window. When it is disabled, owners call the agency. White label recovery lets the agency respond under its own name while Unbanz prepares the appeal behind the scenes. The platform decides, and no outcome is promised.',
 [('Owners are not technical', 'They need someone to explain the notice and start the process.'),
  ('Local reputation', 'A word-of-mouth agency lives on keeping small clients calm.'),
  ('Small jobs add up', 'One recovery job per client per year is still a good reason to be known as the helpful agency.')],
 [('typical', 'Typical local business problems',
   UL(['A Page for a salon or restaurant unpublished with no clear reason.', 'An Instagram account hacked and used to message customers.', 'An ad account disabled after a first boosted post.', 'A business listed under a name the platform considers misleading.']) +
   P('The routes are the same as anywhere. Pages are covered on [[white-label-facebook-page-recovery|white label Facebook Page recovery]].')),
  ('explain', 'Explaining it to a small business owner',
   TMPL('Plain explanation', 'Facebook or Instagram switched off your account. There is an official way to ask them to look again. I will prepare and file it with a specialist, and you will get updates from me. I cannot promise the decision, because they make it. Please do not try to log in over and over and do not share any codes.') +
   P('More wording in [[how-to-tell-client-account-disabled-template|the client template page]].')),
  ('info', 'What to ask the owner for',
   UL(['The business name as it shows on the Page or profile.', 'A screenshot of the notice.', 'The email and phone number used for the account.', 'Proof the business is real, such as a website or registration details, if the platform asks.']) +
   P('Country-specific business details are covered on the [[/white-label-agency/|white label hub]] by country.')),
  ('pricing', 'Pricing for small clients',
   P('Small clients are sensitive to cost and to surprises. Be clear up front about what you do and do not promise. A simple incident fee or a small monthly protection add-on works well. See [[how-to-price-account-recovery-for-agencies|how to price recovery]].')),
  ('local-review', 'Prevention that suits small shops',
   P('Add a second admin, use an authenticator app and keep the recovery email in the owner’s name. Offer to do this setup in a short call. The [[prevent-client-account-lockouts-agency-checklist|checklist]] has the steps.'))],
 [('Is white label recovery suited to local agencies?', 'Yes. Small business owners call their agency when a Page or profile is disabled, and white label lets the agency respond without building the skill.'),
  ('What if the owner cannot find the notice?', 'Ask for any email from the platform and a screenshot of what they see on login. The date and the wording matter.'),
  ('Can you recover a Page without admin access?', 'It depends on the situation. Tell Unbanz exactly what the owner sees and ask. The platform decides.'),
  ('Should I tell the owner a specialist is involved?', 'That is your decision. If the owner asks directly, answer honestly.'),
  ('Does Unbanz contact the owner?', 'No. Unbanz only works with your agency.')],
 REL_AUD + ['white-label-facebook-page-recovery', 'how-to-tell-client-account-disabled-template', 'white-label-recovery-for-web-design-seo-agencies'],
 'Local agencies: small business lockouts, plain explanations and fair pricing.')
